		<p class="csF0A1D375"><span class="cs5925EB86">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375"><span class="cs5925EB86">METHODOLOGY:</span></p><p class="cs9E885863"><span class="csDD5E5F52">This bill sets maximum lot size requirements for single-family residential uses depending on the availability of municipal or community sewer and water infrastructure. &nbsp;For lots without municipal/community sewer, the maximum lot size is set at 66,000 square feet. &nbsp;For those with municipal/community water, the limit is 44,000 square feet, unless there&rsquo;s proof of inadequate water capacity. &nbsp;For lots with municipal/community sewer, the maximum is 22,000 square feet unless there&#39;s proof of inadequate sewer capacity to support more homes.</span></p><p class="cs9E885863"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs9E885863"><span class="csDD5E5F52">The New Hampshire Municipal Association indicates that municipalities will incur indeterminable costs with amending local zoning ordinances, determining what defines a majority of land zoned for residential uses, assessing if the water or sewer system has adequate capacity, and gathering empirical evidence to challenge the lot size requirement.</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375"><span class="cs5925EB86">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375"><span class="cs5925EB86">AGENCIES CONTACTED:</span></p><p class="cs9E885863"><span class="csDD5E5F52">New Hampshire Municipal Association</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="cs2C11128C">&nbsp;</span></p></body>
